We address this gap by decomposing panel vulnerability into two predictors: weakest-link susceptibility ($\eta_{\max}$, the maximum individual attack success rate in a panel) and effective independent channels ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$, a spectral measure of judge independence). Across all 12 conditions (two benchmarks $\times$ six attacks), $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s ordinary least-squares (OLS) coefficient is positive (12/12), directionally consistent with adversarial ensemble theory \citep{goodfellow2015adversarial,papernot2016transferability}. However, the significance of this association is method-sensitive (0 to 12 of 12 across inference approaches), and a previously uncharacterized transition emerges: at $K{=}3$, both predictors contribute (bootstrap significance: $\eta_{\max}$ 3/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 4/12); at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the dominant predictor (7/12 at $K{=}5$, 8/12 at $K{=}7$) while $\eta_{\max}$ sharply attenuates (2/12, then 1/12).

Controlled experiments across 15 models (9 API, 6 local quantized), $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, six attack types, and two benchmarks, Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) and the AI2 Reasoning Challenge (ARC), confirm this predictor shift. Under random faults, panel composition is highly predictive ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$); adaptive attack reduces predictability to $R^2 = 0.275$ (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}).

These findings yield three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Diversity--vulnerability association.} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient direction is positive in all 12 conditions (12/12 under OLS and generalized additive model (GAM) bootstrap), but the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) limits causal interpretation: Cinelli--Hazlett robustness values do not exceed the confound in 69\% of conditions (25/36), though fractional logit confirms the positive association in most conditions ($K{=}3$: 9/12; $K{=}5$: 11/12; $K{=}7$: 12/12) (\S\ref{sec:dominance}, \S\ref{sec:gam_taxonomy}).
\item \textbf{Panel-size-dependent predictor shift.} At $K{=}3$, both $\eta_{\max}$ (3/12) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (4/12) contribute under bootstrap (co-determination); at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the dominant predictor (7/12 at $K{=}5$, 8/12 at $K{=}7$) while $\eta_{\max}$ sharply attenuates (2/12, then 1/12) (\S\ref{sec:cross_k}, \S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}).
\item \textbf{Aggregation as predictability control.} Aggregation rules modulate how much variance panel composition explains ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09). Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), so the defense is partial rather than complete (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

Our findings are descriptive; the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$; \S\ref{sec:analysis}) limits causal claims.

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Predictor Shift and Direction.}
At $K{=}3$, a single compromised judge flips the majority ($t{=}2$), making both predictors relevant. At $K{\geq}5$, no single judge is pivotal; $\eta_{\max}$'s contribution collapses while high-$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ panels present more distinct attack surfaces ($K^* \in (3,5)$). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction is positive in all 12 conditions (OLS and GAM bootstrap 12/12), but significance is method-sensitive (0--12/12; bootstrap 4/12 at $K{=}3$, 7/12 at $K{=}5$). No magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$; Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_keff_eta_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Panel-level correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ at $K{=}5$ ($n{=}3{,}003$ panels). Full model pool (gray, $r{=}0.81$) exhibits strong collinearity; capability-matched Pool~A (orange, $r{=}0.50$, 126 panels) shows reduced confounding.}
\label{fig:keff_eta_scatter}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Diversity--Capability Confound: A Fundamental Limitation.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ correlate at $r{=}0.70$--$0.81$ (variance inflation factor, VIF${\approx}2.0$--$2.9$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}). Cinelli--Hazlett sensitivity analysis \citep{cinelli2020making} shows the confound explains the association in 69\% of conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}), though fractional logit confirms the positive association (9/12, 11/12, 12/12 at $K{=}3,5,7$). Pool~A ($r{=}0.50$) raises the robustness value (RV) in 10/12 at $K{=}5$ despite max 30.1\% power (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_poolA}); cross-benchmark instrumental variable (IV) analysis provides additional identification (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_benchmark}), though causal attribution requires capability-controlled designs.

\paragraph{API-Only vs.\ Full Pool.}
Restricting to 9 API models removes the AWQ confound but increases collinearity ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$), reversing the pattern: $\eta_{\max}$ retains significance (8/12) while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ drops (2--3/12; Table~\ref{tab:robustness_checks}). The six local AWQ models (25--54\% accuracy) drive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ variation but also introduce the confound (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_panel_design.pdf}
\caption{Panel ASR under three selection strategies. $\min(\eta_{\max})$ achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions, confirming robustness-first selection dominates diversity-maximizing strategies.}
\label{fig:panel_design}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_aggregation_gradient.pdf}
\caption{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient distribution across aggregation methods ($K{=}3$). Majority and weighted voting: uniformly positive (12+/0$-$); Bayesian calibration: reduced predictability ($R^2{=}0.087$, 5+/1$-$).}
\label{fig:aggregation}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
Aggregation rules influence predictability but not $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (Figure~\ref{fig:aggregation}); at $K{=}5$ all four methods converge to 7/12, while at $K{=}7$ Bayesian calibration neutralizes it (0/12) as the posterior concentrates on 2--3 accurate judges, compressing $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ variation (Appendices~\ref{app:aggregation_k5},~\ref{app:aggregation_k7}). Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in all conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:panel_design}); clean $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is stable under 4/6 attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}).

\paragraph{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vs.\ Alternative Diversity Measures.}
$\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} outperforms $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ at $K{=}3$ (8/12 vs.\ 4/12) but falls behind at $K{\geq}5$ (6--7/12 vs.\ 7--8/12) without the predictor shift ($8 \to 7 \to 6$; Appendix~\ref{app:alt_diversity}). Shannon MI entropy shows no shift either ($4 \to 4 \to 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:shannon_entropy}).

